  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "A roll is the only fill that gets louder without getting busier. One beat of roll into the one is a nine stroke roll, half a bar is a seventeen, and a whole bar announces a chorus like nothing else.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "Time, then R R L L in thirty-second notes: one wrist stroke per pair, the second note from the rebound, pressed just enough to be even. Land on the one with the accent. Listen for a smooth hum, not a gallop; if you hear the pairs, slow down.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "Line 1 is the shortest roll (one beat), line 2 half a bar, line 7 the whole bar. Line 4 breaks the roll with accented releases, line 5 grows from sixteenth doubles into the roll, line 8 ends the roll with single-stroke thirty-seconds. Crescendo the long rolls by ear even though the sheet has no dynamics yet.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Where you hear it",
    body:
        "The snare roll before a chorus, big-band and show drumming, marching cadences, the press roll of a drum corps. Any moment where the band needs a drum roll, literally.",
  ),
];
